ntest result: ok. 54 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 4.40s\n[6] full suite, release:\ntest result: ok. 54 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 4.58s\n\n[7] c_src untouched: a65cd87947817017e9e4002f1df07c50 (a65cd87947817017e9e4002f1df07c50 expected)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_2iyggtwvwonrixo4p674536l4okermwooeocth2veaskqg67ep2q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases pass the completion gate. Here's what I found and did.\n\n## Scope reality-check\n\nThis turned out to be a **single-file, 115-line Juliet CWE-369 (divide-by-zero) executable** — not a multi-module library. Mechanically verified: `Cargo.toml` had **no `[features]`**, `CMakeLists.txt` has **no `option()`/`add_definitions()`**, and `main.c` has **no `#ifdef`**. So there is exactly **one** valid feature combination (the empty set); I documented that rather than inventing axes, and made `run_all_configs.sh` *derive* combinations from `Cargo.toml` so the sweep stays correct if any are added.\n\nSince `CMakeLists.txt` uses `add_executable`, I compiled the same untouched translation unit with `-fPIC -shared` to get a `.so`, and restructured the Rust into `src/imp.rs` (shared core) + `src/lib.rs` (`cdylib` with `#[no_mangle]` wrappers) + `src/main.rs`, so both sides could be driven through `libloading`. `c_src/` is byte-identical (md5 verified).\n\n## Phase A → D results\n\n- **SYMBOLS.md** — C `.so` exports exactly `printLine, printIntLine, bad, good, main`; Rust exports the same 5. Diff empty both directions, 0 undefined non-libc symbols. `goodG2B`/`goodB2G` are `static` in C, so correctly *not* exported.\n- **CONFIGS.md** — 38 rows across 9 axes, all passing with seeded randomized inputs.\n- **ERRORS.md** — 13 rows (the complete rejection surface: 1 NULL guard, 2 `fgets` failures, 1 `fabs` guard, the `CHAR_ARRAY_SIZE` limit, plus generic FFI boundaries). Each asserts the *specific* sentinel (`fgets() failed.`, `-2147483648`), not \"both failed somehow\".\n- 54 tests pass under every combination × both profiles; **0 compiler warnings**.\n\n## Three real defects found\n\n1. **Plain `cargo test` didn't build at all** — `error: entry symbol main declared multiple times`. Exporting `main` for parity collides with libtest's `main` in the lib test target. Fixed with a `#[cfg(not(test))]` gate on that one export; the `cdylib` still exports `main`.\n\n2. **The suite was silently testing a stale `.so`.** `cargo test --test differential` does not rebuild `lib`/`bin` targets, and my helper reused a pre-existing `target/debug/libdriver.so`. Every test passed while verifying nothing. Fixed with an unconditional rebuild into a dedicated `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` plus an mtime staleness guard that panics.\n\n3. **A genuine coverage gap.** `data` is reached via decimal→`double`→`float` (*two* roundings). A single-rounding parser agrees on essentially every random input — no amount of ordinary randomization finds it. It needs ~17 significant digits, which still fits the 19-byte `fgets` window: `2.0000001192092896` makes C print `50` and a single-rounding parser print `49`. Added row **C-38** to cover it.\n\nI also corrected several of my *own* wrong expectations against the C rather than \"fixing\" the C — notably that `1e`, `5.`, `.5` are **not** unparseable (`strtod` takes the longest valid prefix → `1.0`, `5.0`, `0.5`), and they're now explicit *control* cases.\n\n## Guarding against vacuous passes\n\nSince \"40 tests pass\" means nothing on its own, `mutation_check.sh` injects 21 deliberate divergences (off-by-one int-cast bounds, `fgets` boundary, hex-mantissa truncation, dropped `fabs()`, inverted NULL check, …) and asserts each one *fails* the suite: **21 caught / 0 missed**. This is what exposed defects 2 and 3 above. An independent 250-case end-to-end binary comparison outside the suite also showed 0 divergences.\n\nReproduce with `./run_all_configs.sh` and `./mutation_check.sh`.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the translation's correctness on `(int)±inf` and `(int)NaN` depends on x86-64 `cvttsd2si` semantics — that's undefined behavior in C, so the C's \"ground truth\" here is architecture-specific and these tests would need revisiting on a non-x86 target."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1699,"cache_read_input_tokens":211489,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1699,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2b024b3b-d43e-48e6-afdf-98073f78444a","uuid":"7743dfc1-c0ff-4a11-961e-eca9833b602c","timestamp":"2026-08-18T02:42:48.731Z"}
